{% from "macros.njk" import callout, snippet with context %}

{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>The select is Material's <strong>exposed dropdown menu</strong> — the
     variation filed under <a href="menu.html">Menus</a>, not under text
     fields. It is not an imitation of a dropdown: it is the same
     <code>fl-menu</code>, the same 48px <code>fl-option</code> rows and the
     same state language, anchored to a field box instead of an icon button.
     The field shows the current value; a hidden input carries it into the
     form.</p>
  <h3>Do use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>One choice from <strong>more than ~5 options</strong> — regions,
        countries, sort orders, plan tiers.</li>
    <li>It sits in a form next to text fields — the shared
        <code>fl-field</code> markup keeps the rhythm identical, and the open
        list reads like every other menu in the product.</li>
    <li>The chosen value must be visible above the list while choosing —
        that is the spec's defining behaviour.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Don't use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>2–5 options — that is a
        <a href="selection-controls.html">radio group</a>; visible options
        beat a hidden list.</li>
    <li>You need <strong>search, free text or rich rows</strong> — an
        editable dropdown is a separate M2 variation. Compose a
        <a href="menu.html">menu</a> with a search field, or use a dialog.</li>
    <li>Multiple selection — use a checkbox group, or the
        <a href="#i-native">native select</a> with <code>multiple</code>.</li>
    <li>The page must work <strong>without JavaScript</strong> — the
        <a href="#i-native">native select</a> is the platform control for
        exactly that case.</li>
  </ul>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "One component, two anchors",
    text: "A menu is this panel anchored to a button; a select is the same panel anchored to a field. Row meaning differs (action vs value), the panel does not — that is why they complement each other.",
    href: "menu.html",
    linkText: "Menu"
  }) }}
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
  <div class="docs-anatomy">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 600 250" role="img" aria-label="Select anatomy diagram: a field box with the current value and a chevron, and an open menu below it whose current row is tinted">
      <rect x="1" y="1" width="598" height="248" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#dadce0"/>
      <!-- label -->
      <rect x="40" y="16" width="110" height="10" rx="5" fill="#444746"/>
      <!-- activated field box -->
      <rect x="40" y="36" width="360" height="44" rx="4" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#1a73e8" stroke-width="2"/>
      <!-- current value -->
      <rect x="56" y="53" width="86" height="10" rx="5" fill="#1f1f1f"/>
      <!-- chevron, flipped up -->
      <path d="M364 60 l6 -6 6 6" stroke="#5f6368" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/>
      <!-- panel -->
      <rect x="40" y="80" width="360" height="144" rx="4" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#dadce0"/>
      <!-- rows: current row tinted -->
      <rect x="41" y="136" width="358" height="48" fill="#e8eaed"/>
      <rect x="64" y="104" width="120" height="10" rx="5" fill="#444746"/>
      <rect x="64" y="160" width="90" height="10" rx="5" fill="#1f1f1f"/>
      <rect x="64" y="208" width="100" height="10" rx="5" fill="#444746"/>
      <!-- callouts -->
      <g font-family="system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="700">
        <g><circle cx="28" cy="58" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="28" y="62" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">1</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="420" cy="58" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="420" y="62" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">2</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="420" cy="96" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="420" y="100" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">3</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="28" cy="160" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="28" y="164" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">4</text></g>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>The field box</strong> — owns every pixel of chrome:
          44px, 1px border, 4px radius, and the hover/focus/error/disabled
          states shared with text fields. The open list is the field's
          activated state.</li>
      <li><strong>The anchor and the value</strong> — a real
          <code>&lt;button role="combobox"&gt;</code> filling the box,
          showing the current row's label. The label's <code>for</code>
          points at it, so clicking the label opens the list.</li>
      <li><strong>The panel</strong> — the same <code>fl-menu</code> as every
          action menu: 1px border, 4px radius, 8px padding, 48px rows, no
          shadow. <code>fl-menu--fullwidth</code> attaches it to the field
          and gives it the field's width.</li>
      <li><strong>The current row</strong> — a persistent
          <code>--fl-divider</code> tint, not the <code>menuitemradio</code>
          check glyph (that is the multi-choice idiom).</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <p>The chevron is a 20px mask filled with <code>--fl-icon</code>, 12px from
     the border, and flips in the activated state. It is not drawn into the
     markup, so it follows the token and swaps with
     <code>--fl-select-chevron</code>. The hidden input is not in the
     diagram: it submits the value.</p>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-behavior">Behavior</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One structure:</strong> a <code>fl-field</code> root, a
        <code>fl-field__box</code> holding the anchor and a hidden input, and
        an <code>fl-menu</code> with <code>role="listbox"</code>. The panel is
        a sibling of the box; the browser's top layer means nothing clips it.</li>
    <li><strong>Opening:</strong> click anywhere in the box, click the label,
        press Enter or Space, or ArrowDown/ArrowUp. The list attaches directly
        under the field at the field's width, flips above when there is no
        room below, and caps to the roomier side with its own scrollbar when
        the options are long.</li>
    <li><strong>Activated:</strong> the accent border (filled: the 2px bottom
        indicator), the flipped chevron and the tinted current row. The state
        is read from <code>:popover-open</code>, so it is the platform's
        truth, not a second JS flag.</li>
    <li><strong>Choosing:</strong> click a row or press Enter/Space on it.
        <code>aria-selected</code> moves, the anchor text updates, the hidden
        input takes the row's <code>value</code> (or its text), the list
        closes, focus returns to the field, and an <code>input</code> then a
        <code>change</code> event bubble from the hidden input. Re-choosing
        the current row only closes.</li>
    <li><strong>Keyboard:</strong> ↑/↓ move, Home/End jump, letters
        type-ahead, Escape closes without choosing and returns focus, Tab
        closes and returns focus to the field (a second Tab leaves it).
        Disabled rows are skipped by the arrow cycle and the type-ahead.</li>
    <li><strong>Field states compose:</strong> <code>fl-field--filled</code>
        (tint fill + bottom indicator), <code>fl-field--error</code> /
        <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> (red border, label and helper), and
        <code>disabled</code> on the anchor (dimmed, cannot open). Disable the
        hidden input too if a disabled field must not submit.</li>
    <li><strong>Form reset</strong> restores the hidden input's default
        value and flint.js brings the painted value back with it.</li>
  </ul>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "Choosing needs flint.js",
    text: "The panel opens without JavaScript (popovertarget is HTML), but rows cannot choose and the panel is not anchored, because both need script. For no-JS pages use the native select below — the same field box with the platform's list."
  }) }}
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The anchor is a real <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> carrying the
        select-only combobox pattern: <code>role="combobox"</code>,
        <code>aria-haspopup="listbox"</code>, <code>aria-controls</code>
        pointing at the panel, and <code>aria-expanded</code> (synced by
        flint.js). Keeping the button element means label clicks, Enter/Space
        and the platform's activation all still work.</li>
    <li>Name the anchor with the <em>label only</em>:
        <code>aria-labelledby="label-id"</code> announces "Workspace type"
        and the browser exposes the visible text as the combobox's value
        ("Team"), so the value is not repeated in the name. A
        <code>&lt;label for&gt;</code> pointing at a button is valid and
        makes the label open the list; keep both, or omit <code>for</code>
        if you only want the <code>aria-labelledby</code> name.</li>
    <li>The panel is <code>role="listbox"</code> named by the label; rows are
        <code>&lt;button role="option"&gt;</code> with
        <code>aria-selected</code>. Focus moves into the list, so assistive
        tech reads the real option under the keyboard — no
        <code>aria-activedescendant</code> bookkeeping.</li>
    <li>Escape closes and returns focus; choosing returns focus to the field.
        The list is not a tab stop: Tab closes it.</li>
    <li><strong>Required is yours to enforce.</strong> A hidden input is
        barred from constraint validation, so <code>required</code> on it
        does nothing. Put <code>aria-required="true"</code> on the combobox
        anchor — valid on <code>role="combobox"</code>, unlike on a plain
        button — and run the check in the <a href="#i-code">code recipe</a>;
        the error state is the field's own <code>aria-invalid</code> styling.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-gaps">Known gaps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>JavaScript is required to choose.</strong> The closed field
        renders identically without it; the list opens but cannot select.
        The <a href="#i-native">native select</a> is the no-JS path.</li>
    <li><strong>No native constraint validation.</strong>
        <code>required</code> is author-checked (see above). Frameworks can
        own this in their wrapper.</li>
    <li><strong>Popover API floor:</strong> Chrome/Edge 114, Safari 17,
        Firefox 125 — the same floor as the menu. Older browsers keep the
        list closed; the closed field is unchanged.</li>
    <li><strong>No grouped options.</strong> <code>optgroup</code> has no
        listbox equivalent here yet; use the native select when groups are
        load-bearing.</li>
    <li><strong>Not an editable dropdown.</strong> Free-text entry ("type a
        value not in the list") is a separate M2 variation and not shipped.</li>
    <li><strong>Static label.</strong> The label sits above the box, like
        every Flint field; M2's exposed dropdown floats the label into the
        border. This is the library's recorded field deviation.</li>
    <li><strong>Mobile shows the in-page list</strong>, not the OS picker.
        M2 permits the native picker on touch; Flint keeps one structure at
        every width.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endset %}

